  "question": {
    "text": "In the electrical network shown in the figure, What will be the potential difference (in volt) across <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math> resistance?<br />\r<br />\r<img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\qt0045_3bb10dd6d8f5.jpg\" style=\"max-width:100%; object-fit:contain\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 169px;\" />"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "24",
  "solution": {
    "text": "As <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> resistances are<br /><br />In parallel, their equivalent resistance will be 2&nbsp;<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math>. Here 2&nbsp;<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> and 4&nbsp;<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> are in series, their equivalent resistance will be 6&nbsp;<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math>. From current distribution law<br /><br /><img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\st0045_11a74d5b3ed9.jpg\" style=\"max-width:100%; object-fit:contain\" style=\"width: 305px; height: 152px;\" /><br /><br /><math><msub><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>18</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>12&#160;</mn><mn>A</mn><mi>&#160;</mi></math><br /><br /><math><msub><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>18</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>6&#160;</mn><mn>A</mn></math><br /><br /><br /><br /><img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\st0045_194243d8a6ac.jpg\" style=\"max-width:100%; object-fit:contain\" style=\"width: 284px; height: 146px;\" /><br /><br /><br /><br />Now, 12 A current is entering in the parallel combination of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> and <math><mn>6&#160;</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> again from current distribution law<br /><br /><math><msubsup><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>&#8242;</mi><mi>&#8242;</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>8&#160;</mn><mn>A</mn><mi>&#160;</mi></math><br /><br /><math><msubsup><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>&#8242;</mi><mi>&#8242;</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>9</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>4&#160;</mn><mn>A</mn><mo>.</mo></math><br /><br />&there4; The potential difference across <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> resistance<br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>"
  }
